WEBVTT

00:02.023 --> 00:05.359
- [narrator] Previously on Lost:
- Hello there. I'm Damon Lindelof.

00:05.443 --> 00:10.531
- And I'm Carlton Cuse.
- And this is Michael Emerson.

00:10.615 --> 00:15.369
[Cuse] And we're all here to talk about
episode 320, The Man Behind the Curtain.

00:15.453 --> 00:19.540
- [Emerson] The Man Behind the Curtain.
- [Lindelof] When you got this script,

00:19.624 --> 00:23.002
and you saw that title, was it
your immediate impression that you

00:23.085 --> 00:26.797
- would be the man behind the curtain?
- [Emerson] I didn't know. I never know

00:26.881 --> 00:30.468
answers to those things.
It encouraged me to read on.

00:30.551 --> 00:33.930
[Cuse] Were you worried that
the implication might be

00:34.013 --> 00:36.474
that there was a weak and meek guy

00:36.557 --> 00:41.062
hiding underneath the veneer
of Benjamin Linus?

00:41.145 --> 00:43.898
[Emerson] That would have been
too much of a changeup.

00:43.981 --> 00:48.319
[Lindelof] Had you gotten any word
that this was your flashback episode?

00:48.402 --> 00:51.364
[Emerson] I... I can't remember.
Jack Bender, maybe,

00:51.447 --> 00:53.574
said I should be
on the lookout for one,

00:53.658 --> 00:59.622
but I didn't believe it 'cause it seemed
like it was such a huge thing.

00:59.705 --> 01:03.084
- Oh, gosh, there's my wife.
- [Lindelof] We start, and obviously,

01:03.167 --> 01:07.255
there's a very significant tidbit here
to reveal to the DVD audience.

01:07.338 --> 01:10.716
- Michael, why don't you do that?
- [Emerson] This is my real-life wife.

01:10.800 --> 01:12.260
[Cuse] Playing his mother.

01:12.343 --> 01:15.263
[Emerson] Giving birth
to my character on TV.

01:15.346 --> 01:19.850
[Cuse] By the way, at Michael's request.
We're trying to process the implications

01:19.934 --> 01:23.562
of why he wanted his wife
to play his mother in Lost.

01:23.646 --> 01:28.901
But we felt we certainly had
to accommodate that for practical

01:28.985 --> 01:32.780
- and psychological reasons.
- [Emerson] And who knows...?

01:32.863 --> 01:38.119
The Freudian ripples of this
are gonna bleed into our lives, I fear.

01:38.202 --> 01:41.455
And here's the little baby covered
in yogurt and strawberry jam.

01:41.539 --> 01:43.833
[Lindelof] Don't wanna know
what we went through

01:43.916 --> 01:45.626
to cast a baby that looked like you.

01:45.710 --> 01:49.297
- It was not easy.
- [Cuse] Looked at thousands of infants.

01:49.380 --> 01:51.757
[Lindelof] We flew this baby
in from New Zealand.

01:51.841 --> 01:53.134
[Cuse] Yes.

01:53.217 --> 01:56.554
[Emerson] I saw this baby
in a liquor store about a week later,

01:56.637 --> 02:00.141
and took my picture with the baby
and the parents.

02:00.224 --> 02:03.144
[Lindelof] Don't wanna know what
he was doing in a liquor store.

02:03.227 --> 02:06.063
[Cuse] It goes back to that
age-old trouble of child actors.

02:06.147 --> 02:09.150
You have a little brief success
and then it leads to demise.

02:09.233 --> 02:12.153
- [Emerson] That's right.
- [Lindelof] This episode, by the way,

02:12.236 --> 02:15.072
was written by Drew Goddard
and Elizabeth Sarnoff.

02:15.156 --> 02:19.327
And one of our favorites of the season,
directed by Bobby Roth, who had not

02:19.410 --> 02:21.662
directed any episodes of Lost
prior to this.

02:21.746 --> 02:24.665
- [Emerson] We had a good time.
- [Cuse] The big reveal here

02:24.749 --> 02:28.753
was we were trying to fool the audience
to thinking this birth was taking place

02:28.836 --> 02:34.050
on the island and not the real world,
till we rushed into this highway

02:34.133 --> 02:38.512
and realized that we're 32 miles
outside of Portland, Oregon. And, uh...

02:38.596 --> 02:42.850
These other characters are gonna emerge,
Samantha Mathis...

02:42.933 --> 02:46.312
- [Lindelof] And Doug Hutchison.
- [Cuse] Who you'll probably see again

02:46.395 --> 02:50.900
in the future of Lost, who are important
members of the Dharma Initiative.

02:50.983 --> 02:54.445
And we are getting a little piece
of their origin story as we are also,

02:54.528 --> 02:59.533
most significantly, getting
the origin story of Benjamin Linus.

02:59.617 --> 03:02.536
- Emily!
- [Lindelof] And here we have

03:02.620 --> 03:06.540
the first significant tragedy
that Ben suffers in his life,

03:06.624 --> 03:10.628
which is the death of his mother,
and here he is.

03:10.711 --> 03:13.589
- [Emerson] Fast forward.
- [Lindelof] Exactly.

03:13.672 --> 03:18.803
[Cuse] So, Michael, what do you do
to prepare when you get a script?

03:18.886 --> 03:21.347
Can you take us through your process?

03:21.430 --> 03:24.850
[Emerson] I just... I just do
the normal actor thing, I think.

03:24.934 --> 03:29.897
I read it through for sense.
And then I try to think

03:29.980 --> 03:33.109
where the script goes
that I've never gone before.

03:33.192 --> 03:37.571
I try to sound all that out in my mind.
"What would it be like if I did this

03:37.655 --> 03:39.740
or said this?"

03:39.824 --> 03:44.912
[Cuse] Do you try on different choices
for how you might play a moment?

03:44.995 --> 03:50.626
Certainly, one of the things
that we love when we watch dailies

03:50.709 --> 03:55.256
and scenes that you're in
is the choices always seem

03:55.339 --> 03:58.509
both completely believable,
yet, in many cases,

03:58.592 --> 04:03.806
not quite what we expected, which
is the mark of a really talented actor.

04:03.889 --> 04:09.103
[Emerson] I'm glad to hear you say that.
I try to come in with a basic choice

04:09.186 --> 04:14.775
for every beat and then we have so many
takes that I get to play around

04:14.859 --> 04:18.571
with alternatives in the course
of the day's work.

04:18.654 --> 04:22.908
[Lindelof] Gotta be tricky on a show
like ours where, taking case in point,

04:22.992 --> 04:25.828
in this scene you have
a line where you say to Richard,

04:25.911 --> 04:28.539
"You do remember birthdays, do you?
Don't you?"

04:28.622 --> 04:31.959
You make a choice on how to play
that because you don't know where we,

04:32.042 --> 04:34.962
the writers...
what the implications of that line is.

04:35.045 --> 04:39.884
[Emerson] It's true. And we, very often,
an ambiguous line like that,

04:39.967 --> 04:42.511
we may do it three or four
different ways.

04:42.595 --> 04:44.805
And then let someone
make the choice in post.

04:44.889 --> 04:46.932
[Lindelof] We pick the way that makes
the most sense. Sometimes the way

04:46.932 --> 04:48.142
[Lindelof] We pick the way that makes
the most sense. Sometimes the way

04:48.225 --> 04:51.645
that you deliver the line actually
influences the storytelling.

04:51.729 --> 04:56.066
[Cuse] Great thing about this episode
is that it really, for the first time,

04:56.150 --> 05:00.988
focuses the show on the showdown
between Locke and Ben

05:01.071 --> 05:03.991
for this kind of title
of master of the island.

05:04.074 --> 05:08.662
And it's not... It certainly isn't
the last chapter in the story,

05:08.746 --> 05:12.124
but it's the first significant one
where we begin to realize

05:12.208 --> 05:17.087
that Locke, you know, is special.
Now, people knew that in some sense,

05:17.171 --> 05:19.507
he was in a wheelchair,
crashed on this island

05:19.590 --> 05:23.677
and then suddenly was able to walk.
Went from being sort of a...

05:23.761 --> 05:27.598
...kind of an ineffectual guy
who was working in a box company

05:27.681 --> 05:32.269
to a hunter and a real
sort of man of the jungle.

05:32.353 --> 05:36.857
And obviously, we know that Ben
is the ostensible leader of the Others.

05:36.941 --> 05:40.528
And this episode really is focused on...
you know, the...

05:40.611 --> 05:43.948
...Locke's kind of

05:44.031 --> 05:48.285
first efforts to threaten Ben's primacy
and what Ben's reaction to that is.

05:48.369 --> 05:49.370
[Emerson] Yeah.

05:49.453 --> 05:52.498
[Lindelof] And obviously,
watching this scene, for us,

05:52.581 --> 05:56.877
it seems to me, at least personally,
that Ben is absolutely convinced

05:56.961 --> 05:58.963
that Jacob is a very real person.

05:59.046 --> 06:03.467
And that none of this is ridiculous to
him. The fact that he's the only person

06:03.551 --> 06:07.721
who can talk to him or see him or hear
him. In the watching of this episode,

06:07.805 --> 06:11.058
especially on a second viewing,
when the audience is watching this

06:11.141 --> 06:15.229
the first time there's this great
mystery hanging over who is Jacob.

06:15.312 --> 06:19.316
They're gonna pull some trick on us.
They can't take us to see him here.

06:19.400 --> 06:22.069
- The show always stalls.
- [Emerson] Right.

06:22.152 --> 06:25.823
[Lindelof] Locke is actually a proxy
for the audience in this scene.

06:25.906 --> 06:29.201
And the scenes that follow it,
which is, "I'm tired of waiting.

06:29.285 --> 06:33.122
I'm tired of these oblique references.
I wanna see."

06:33.205 --> 06:36.750
So, obviously, kind of,
this entire story line

06:36.834 --> 06:39.420
is focused on this cabin scene,

06:39.503 --> 06:41.672
which we'll talk a lot about
when we get there.

06:41.755 --> 06:44.383
Really curious about choices
you made in that scene.

06:49.013 --> 06:52.433
- [Cuse] Here we see young Ben arriving.
- [Emerson] There's young Ben.

06:52.516 --> 06:55.936
[Lindelof] This is interesting because
this is the first time on the show

06:56.020 --> 06:58.939
that we've had the bulk
of the flashbacks for a character

06:59.023 --> 07:03.819
played by another actor.
Did you meet or talk to this actor?

07:03.902 --> 07:08.616
[Emerson] Yeah, I saw him several times
at lunch. He was a real nice fellow

07:08.699 --> 07:10.784
and really highly professional.

07:10.868 --> 07:16.290
[Lindelof] We were really impressed
by him. Child actors always sort of

07:16.373 --> 07:19.209
make the mistake of acting
as opposed to doing.

07:19.293 --> 07:23.464
But there was something very natural
about this kid's approach. I liked him.

07:23.547 --> 07:28.302
[Emerson] He was a real trooper.
Take after take, he was interested

07:28.385 --> 07:32.264
- in giving the director what he needed.
- [Cuse] What's interesting, we...

07:32.348 --> 07:36.644
One of the things we do on Lost
which is different than other shows is,

07:36.727 --> 07:42.316
when we come up with an idea
for a character, we reverse-engineer it.

07:42.399 --> 07:45.861
We knew we had this character who
was gonna be the leader of the Others

07:45.944 --> 07:50.491
who we were going to, in season two,
have caught by our guys

07:50.574 --> 07:53.702
and become a prisoner of war,
was how we were referring to him.

07:53.786 --> 07:57.665
We would hold him captive in the hatch
and then he would eventually escape

07:57.748 --> 08:01.835
and we'd be surprised to later learn
that the guy we were actually holding,

08:01.919 --> 08:03.837
the guy right under our noses,

08:03.921 --> 08:07.549
was the leader of this other group
of people that lived on the island.

08:07.633 --> 08:09.927
Damon and I were sitting in my office,

08:10.010 --> 08:13.180
we were talking about actors,
'cause we sort of, we...

08:13.263 --> 08:16.183
In this reverse-engineering
process for us, the idea is,

08:16.266 --> 08:19.853
let's find an actor we like,
and then we have a concept for the role,

08:19.937 --> 08:24.191
but we'll then tailor the role
to the actor who we like.

08:24.274 --> 08:27.986
And, you know, we had both
seen Michael in The Practice,

08:28.070 --> 08:31.323
and he had played
a very chilling serial killer

08:31.407 --> 08:36.412
in an arc of episodes on that show,
and won an Emmy for his performance.

08:36.495 --> 08:40.124
And we were like,
"What about Michael Emerson?"

08:40.207 --> 08:42.418
And we were like,
"Yeah, he would be great.

08:42.501 --> 08:45.671
I wonder where he is."
And we got in touch with April Webster,

08:45.754 --> 08:49.550
our casting director, and she said
Michael was in New York and available.

08:49.633 --> 08:53.011
I remember we got on the phone
with Michael, and it was funny,

08:53.095 --> 08:57.141
because it was like, as described,
a sort of slushy day in New York.

08:57.224 --> 09:00.978
And he was walking along the street,
and we were, like, "Want to go to Hawaii

09:01.061 --> 09:03.230
- for a couple of episodes?"
- [Emerson] Yes.

09:03.313 --> 09:06.692
[Cuse] There wasn't even a pause.
There wasn't even a hesitation.

09:06.775 --> 09:08.360
It was like, "Absolutely."

09:08.444 --> 09:10.988
[Emerson] Yeah, it was snowing
in New York.

09:11.071 --> 09:13.991
[Lindelof] It was the easy sell.
And obviously, you know,

09:14.074 --> 09:18.245
as... we had hoped it would work out,
but you never know.

09:18.328 --> 09:21.749
You could have had
an absolute miserable time down there,

09:21.832 --> 09:24.835
or it might not have clicked.
But it came together

09:24.918 --> 09:28.172
in such a beautiful way that,
you know, in many ways I think Ben

09:28.255 --> 09:32.885
and certainly Juliet became the
real story drive of the entire season.

09:32.968 --> 09:35.554
When you look at these episodes,
one after the other,

09:35.637 --> 09:39.933
with the exception of one or two,
they're really the characters

09:40.017 --> 09:43.520
that are creating the conflict
at the heart of every single episode.

09:43.604 --> 09:47.232
[Emerson] A couple of really interesting
characters, I have to say.

09:47.316 --> 09:51.028
[Cuse] It's kind of hard now to imagine
how we motored along for, you know,

09:51.111 --> 09:54.782
a season and a half
without an antagonist on the show.

09:54.865 --> 09:57.659
[Lindelof] Well, they were all
fighting with each other.

09:57.743 --> 10:01.455
But just at the point
where that was seeming to get old,

10:01.538 --> 10:05.751
we kind of went to Ben. So everything
has worked out rather nicely.

10:05.834 --> 10:10.005
[Cuse] I assume in your own brain
you think Ben is actually probably

10:10.088 --> 10:12.216
the most noble guy
on the entire island.

10:12.299 --> 10:16.470
[Emerson] Well, I'm still holding out,
and I don't think without cause,

10:16.553 --> 10:20.390
to this idea that he's
the only one in the know.

10:20.474 --> 10:25.103
He knows the scale of the dilemma here

10:25.187 --> 10:30.526
and that there are... that there are
even worse powers out there.

10:30.609 --> 10:34.488
[Lindelof] Well, you know, now that
we've seen the finale, obviously,

10:34.571 --> 10:38.325
you know, for everything Ben does
in the finale that seems despicable,

10:38.408 --> 10:41.954
like shooting Bernard, Sayid and Jin,

10:42.037 --> 10:45.040
which, in fact, we realize
that he gave the order not to do that,

10:45.123 --> 10:48.502
that it was a coercive tact.
Then the other thing he says to Jack,

10:48.585 --> 10:51.922
is this woman is not who she
says she is, turns out to be validated,

10:52.005 --> 10:55.759
at least on some level by what
Penny says to Charlie in the finale,

10:55.843 --> 10:59.179
that she doesn't know who this woman is
or anything about this boat.

10:59.263 --> 11:01.640
So coming out of the season,
you're kind of...

11:01.723 --> 11:05.185
Hopefully, the audience is feeling
some degree of sympathy for Ben

11:05.269 --> 11:07.521
'cause he took a beating
at Jack's hands.

11:07.604 --> 11:11.149
Maybe deserved. I mean,
when you pretend to kill three people,

11:11.233 --> 11:15.863
you should at least get a couple
bruises. But, you know,

11:15.946 --> 11:21.702
coming into the next year, he will
have won a little bit of credulity back,

11:21.785 --> 11:26.456
- once what he said proves to be true.
- [Cuse] Well, yeah. I mean, if it does.

11:26.540 --> 11:30.836
Certainly, the audience maybe
doesn't believe

11:30.919 --> 11:35.883
Ben's point of view
is very defensible now,

11:35.966 --> 11:40.053
but it's entirely possible that,
looking back on it, we'll go,

11:40.137 --> 11:43.724
"Jack should have listened to Ben.
He should never have made that call."

11:43.807 --> 11:46.810
[Lindelof] I love this scene
because it reminds me of this movie,

11:46.894 --> 11:49.897
My Bodyguard, that I loved
growing up, where basically

11:49.980 --> 11:54.192
Chris Makepeace plays this kid
who gets picked on by Matt Dillon,

11:54.276 --> 11:58.989
and Matt Dillon hires a bodyguard
and Chris Makepeace hires a bodyguard,

11:59.072 --> 12:02.326
and the bodyguards duke it out.
Chris Makepeace just watches.

12:02.409 --> 12:06.622
I love that Locke doesn't beat up Ben.
He beats up somebody in front of Ben

12:06.705 --> 12:08.707
to sort of make his point.

12:08.790 --> 12:10.500
For it to ever get physical
between those two guys,

12:10.500 --> 12:11.668
For it to ever get physical
between those two guys,

12:11.752 --> 12:14.296
I think it would be cheap.

12:14.379 --> 12:17.716
[Cuse] I don't know, I'd like to see...
l was watching this scene

12:17.799 --> 12:21.887
the other day where Ben takes his
little extendo-stick onto Sawyer.

12:21.970 --> 12:24.932
And I'm thinking,
"If we gave Locke a weapon

12:25.015 --> 12:28.894
and we had Ben with his extendo-stick,
that could be a good battle sequence."

12:28.977 --> 12:31.688
- [Emerson] Could be interesting.
- [Lindelof] Here's Alex.

12:31.772 --> 12:35.192
[Cuse] What's really interesting
about Ben in this episode,

12:35.275 --> 12:41.114
is that we sort of reveal this... The
two women who are important in his life.

12:41.198 --> 12:46.620
One of them obviously is his daughter,
or at least,

12:46.703 --> 12:50.290
while maybe not his biological daughter,
he has raised this girl,

12:50.374 --> 12:52.876
and yet there's obviously a lot...

12:52.960 --> 12:55.128
There's an enormous
sort of tension and...

12:55.212 --> 12:58.465
[Lindelof] By the way,
in the original version of the script,

12:58.548 --> 13:01.802
Ben was supposed to say,
"Gee, I was expecting a cake."

13:01.885 --> 13:05.138
And we actually shot that. But
in the moment, in the cutting room,

13:05.222 --> 13:10.102
we lost it because
it just felt, like, so cavalier.

13:10.185 --> 13:13.271
And that's a case where sometimes
you did your best,

13:13.355 --> 13:16.024
but it was the writing,
our decision to do that,

13:16.108 --> 13:20.028
and forcing Drew and Liz to put it in
the script. And it just didn't work.

13:20.112 --> 13:22.823
- [Cuse] Yeah.
- [Emerson] I half expected it to go.

13:22.906 --> 13:26.827
[Cuse] And here's the other
important woman in his life.

13:26.910 --> 13:31.331
And that is Annie, who is
his fellow classmate. There she is.

13:31.415 --> 13:36.294
You know, this Annie is gonna prove
to be very significant in Ben's life.

13:36.378 --> 13:39.548
[Emerson] I was gonna say,
I bet we haven't heard the last of her.

13:39.631 --> 13:43.844
[Lindelof] Huge, huge part. And
obviously, there's a little Easter egg

13:43.927 --> 13:46.513
that we caught here.
Olivia, the teacher there,

13:46.596 --> 13:50.183
mentions a volcano on the island,

13:50.267 --> 13:54.271
which will be slightly
less important than Annie,

13:54.354 --> 13:56.982
but still seismic,
if you'll pardon the pun.

13:57.065 --> 14:01.319
[Cuse] We like this scene, too,
because it was sort of, like, you know,

14:01.403 --> 14:05.157
kind of a great M. Night Shyamalan
scene, like Signs,

14:05.240 --> 14:09.369
where you kind of imply something
that's going on offstage,

14:09.453 --> 14:13.373
and allow the audience's imagination
to work, as opposed to illustrating it.

14:13.457 --> 14:17.377
So the notion that there's this
skirmish between the Dharma Initiative

14:17.461 --> 14:19.421
and the hostiles,
and all we do is hear it,

14:19.504 --> 14:23.383
and we see sort of a well-trained
routine and a classmate where

14:23.467 --> 14:27.220
an elementary school teacher locks
a gun with sort of great proficiency

14:27.304 --> 14:30.390
implies a lot about
what the heck has been going on here

14:30.474 --> 14:32.142
with Dharma and the Others.

14:32.225 --> 14:36.063
[Lindelof] Samantha was really, really
good in this episode, as was Doug.

14:36.146 --> 14:39.566
It's amazing how guest cast,
they basically come in,

14:39.649 --> 14:41.693
they work for three or four days,

14:41.777 --> 14:44.237
what an amazing impression
these two actors made,

14:44.321 --> 14:48.408
which is good news for us as writers
because Horace and Olivia

14:48.492 --> 14:52.829
actually factor very significantly
in the game plan coming down the road.

14:52.913 --> 14:57.751
My wife screamed when we watched this
episode together, right about here.

14:57.834 --> 15:01.338
[Cuse] We we wanted this to be
a complete horror movie moment.

15:01.421 --> 15:06.593
We talked to Bobby Roth about it,
and we talked a lot in the writers' room

15:06.676 --> 15:09.721
about really designing this
as a real "boo" moment,

15:09.805 --> 15:13.767
where we wanted the audience
to be utterly shocked, like, you know,

15:13.850 --> 15:17.104
if you were watching The Ring
or something like that,

15:17.187 --> 15:22.067
and you all of a sudden
see this image in the window.

15:22.150 --> 15:25.237
Then obviously the follow-up here
was to really establish,

15:25.320 --> 15:28.615
in this picture,
that this was Ben's mother out there,

15:28.698 --> 15:31.743
for people who might, you know,
not have connected...

15:31.827 --> 15:34.955
[Lindelof] Not have remembered her
from the throes of labor.

15:35.038 --> 15:38.750
[Emerson] One thing I like about this
episode is it has a couple of really

15:38.834 --> 15:41.044
good horror-movie moments in it.

15:41.128 --> 15:43.463
[Lindelof] It's really cool.
Totally freaky.

15:43.547 --> 15:47.217
[Emerson] It's a little more
haunted than Lost usually is.

15:47.300 --> 15:49.636
- I like that about it.
- [Lindelof] The cabin scene

15:49.719 --> 15:52.305
is very psychologically
disturbing because...

15:52.389 --> 15:56.226
...it's not the part where everything
starts to go haywire that's so scary.

15:56.309 --> 15:58.228
It's the part where Ben is having

15:58.311 --> 16:03.275
a perfectly rational explanation...
conversation with a chair.

16:03.358 --> 16:07.571
[Cuse] It's kind of like the best
of Hitchcock kind of moment there.

16:07.654 --> 16:09.656
It was just a great acting moment.

16:09.739 --> 16:13.869
That whole sequence, we'll talk about it
a little bit more when we get to it.

16:13.952 --> 16:16.913
So how was it adjusting
to life in Hawaii

16:16.997 --> 16:20.375
after being a long-time New Yorker?

16:20.458 --> 16:25.839
[Emerson] Well, it's... Everywhere else
you go you have to slow down a little.

16:25.922 --> 16:30.093
But the weather is agreeable
and the people are so friendly

16:30.177 --> 16:35.098
and hospitable there. It's sort of
wonderful that we film on that island,

16:35.182 --> 16:37.642
because the tone of the crew

16:37.726 --> 16:41.646
is so laid back
and constructive, I'd say.

16:41.730 --> 16:44.774
I've never worked on a show where

16:44.858 --> 16:47.694
everybody seemed to be
pulling in the same direction.

16:47.777 --> 16:51.781
And everyone was so interested
in helping each other out.

16:51.865 --> 16:55.702
- It's really good that way.
- [Cuse] When you walk into a place

16:55.785 --> 16:58.371
and people recognize you
as Benjamin Linus,

16:58.455 --> 17:01.625
what kind of reactions do you get,
publicly?

17:01.708 --> 17:03.960
[Emerson] Well, people are
sort of pleased,

17:04.044 --> 17:07.756
I think, in a way, to confuse the actor
and the character a little bit.

17:07.839 --> 17:10.425
- [Lindelof] Right.
- [Emerson] But it plays out nicely

17:10.508 --> 17:14.095
in the sense that people are
more formal with me than they might be

17:14.179 --> 17:18.183
with Jorge or Josh. They tend
to keep their distance a little bit.

17:18.183 --> 17:18.767
With Jorge or Josh. They tend
to keep their distance a little bit.

17:18.850 --> 17:23.563
And are a little more wary with me,
which is fine.

17:23.647 --> 17:27.651
- [Cuse] Do you still get good tables?
- [Emerson] I'm doing all right.

17:27.734 --> 17:32.072
[Lindelof] That's all that matters,
at the end of the day, is being seen.

17:32.155 --> 17:35.242
[Cuse] Um... Nobody comes up to you...
Do people come up to you

17:35.325 --> 17:38.745
- and say, "You're a bad man"?
- [Emerson] They do that all the time.

17:38.828 --> 17:40.997
[Lindelof] And that was
before he got the show.

17:41.081 --> 17:42.707
[Cuse] Surprisingly.

17:42.791 --> 17:47.420
[Emerson] "Oh, you're scary. You're
awful. Oh, I hate you, I hate you."

17:47.504 --> 17:51.299
[Lindelof] And then you just have to nod
and say, "Thank you very much, madam."

17:51.383 --> 17:54.678
- [Emerson] "Thank you very much, Mom."
- [Lindelof] Yeah, exactly.

17:54.761 --> 17:57.222
- Wonderful.
- [Cuse] So these dolls

17:57.305 --> 18:00.308
were a bit of... kind of iconography,

18:00.392 --> 18:04.437
which we wanted to kind of establish
to kind of show the bond.

18:04.521 --> 18:06.648
And the fact that Ben keeps this doll

18:06.731 --> 18:09.651
that Annie has carved for him
for his entire life.

18:09.734 --> 18:13.446
[Lindelof] We also liked the idea,
and I think this was Liz Sarnoff's idea,

18:13.530 --> 18:17.492
that they were on that swing set that
Ben would later have Sayid chained to.

18:17.575 --> 18:19.244
- And, you know...
- [Emerson] Oh.

18:19.327 --> 18:23.123
[Lindelof] That idea of this was just
a beautiful, sweet childhood moment.

18:23.206 --> 18:28.253
But later, when he moves into
New Otherton and sort of takes it over

18:28.336 --> 18:32.299
and becomes the leader, that swing set
is no longer used for kids,

18:32.382 --> 18:36.678
because they can't have kids.
The only purpose that it would serve

18:36.761 --> 18:40.473
is to restrain somebody.
I thought that was a nice touch.

18:40.557 --> 18:44.811
[Emerson] Now, Lost does a good job
of presenting a thing as benign,

18:44.894 --> 18:49.566
and then revisiting it later
when it has a scarier dimension.

18:49.649 --> 18:52.819
- [Lindelof] Like the Dharma van.
- [Emerson] Yeah. How about that.

18:52.902 --> 18:58.074
[Cuse] A lot of people claim that
we have some father issues on Lost.

18:58.158 --> 18:59.617
[Emerson laughs]

18:59.701 --> 19:03.788
[Lindelof] I think this is a very sweet
scene between a father and a son.

19:03.872 --> 19:07.334
[Cuse] We never thought this was
an unhealthy father-son relationship

19:07.417 --> 19:10.754
until we actually read the press
after the episode, so maybe...

19:10.837 --> 19:13.423
Maybe we're the ones with the problem.
I don't know.

19:13.506 --> 19:16.509
[Lindelof] Well, I think this
is an incredibly important scene.

19:16.593 --> 19:21.598
I think, A, it's beautifully acted,
but also he has to say this

19:21.681 --> 19:26.770
in order to understand why Ben does
what he does 20-some-odd years later.

19:26.853 --> 19:30.190
I think if those words
didn't come out of his mouth,

19:30.273 --> 19:34.986
you would feel like Ben was more
of a monster for killing his father.

19:35.070 --> 19:38.990
That sort of emotional trauma, I feel,
is about a thousand times worse

19:39.074 --> 19:42.452
than him hauling off
and backhanding him across the face.

19:43.536 --> 19:44.913
[Emerson] Yeah.

19:44.996 --> 19:47.832
[Lindelof] Here, Carrie comes back.
I like her in this scene.

19:47.916 --> 19:51.378
I'm not saying that 'cause
you're married to her. She's just there.

19:51.461 --> 19:55.131
[Cuse] That dress choice. That must be
some Jack Bender fantasy. I don't know.

19:55.215 --> 19:56.216
[Lindelof] Weird.

19:56.299 --> 19:58.885
[Emerson] I love these long shots,
like this. It's cool.

19:58.968 --> 20:02.222
[Lindelof] Obviously, only half
those pylons are actually there.

20:02.305 --> 20:05.392
The rest of them are done
by our crack visual effects squad.

20:05.475 --> 20:09.229
- And by "crack" I mean smoking crack.
- [Emerson laughs]

20:09.312 --> 20:13.066
[Cuse] he thing that's cool about
the island is it can conjure up these...

20:13.149 --> 20:17.654
...these images of great importance
from your life,

20:17.737 --> 20:22.200
and then the characters on the show have
to sort of deal with the consequences.

20:22.283 --> 20:26.204
And those visions often provide tests

20:26.287 --> 20:29.958
right along the axis of what's
most important for the character.

20:30.041 --> 20:32.836
[Lindelof] To get that performance
out of that young man,

20:32.919 --> 20:35.630
before we rolled,
Jack Bender went to him and told him

20:35.713 --> 20:38.216
that we had kidnapped his mother.

20:38.299 --> 20:40.885
And then afterwards,
he said, "Just kidding."

20:40.969 --> 20:43.847
[Cuse] It was unkind,
but sometimes in the service of art,

20:43.930 --> 20:47.100
- you have to do these things.
- [Lindelof] OK, here we come to the...

20:47.183 --> 20:52.063
We do have to talk a little bit about
what you thought you were stepping over.

20:52.147 --> 20:56.568
[Cuse] Did you get any explanation from
Jack about the mysterious circle of ash?

20:56.651 --> 20:59.779
[Emerson] I didn't really. I assumed
it was a kind of a witch circle.

20:59.863 --> 21:03.908
[Cuse] Yeah. I mean, there's a bit
of kind of protection or magic.

21:03.992 --> 21:05.952
Or, you know, kind of containment.

21:06.035 --> 21:08.788
In a certain way, part
of the idea here, I think...

21:08.872 --> 21:12.208
- [Lindelof] He's afraid to touch it.
- [Cuse] Ben has powers over Jacob,

21:12.292 --> 21:16.421
and Jacob has some powers over Ben.
And there's a bit of a stasis.

21:16.504 --> 21:20.592
But, obviously, on a bigger picture
level, what we really wanted to do

21:20.675 --> 21:24.220
was once Ben kind of recovers
from his surprise

21:24.304 --> 21:26.973
that Locke was able
to carry through on this task

21:27.056 --> 21:31.227
that Ben never thought he would be able
to complete, that is, kill his father,

21:31.311 --> 21:35.356
now Ben really wants to find out,
"Is Locke special?

21:35.440 --> 21:39.152
Is he actually communing with the island
in a significant way,

21:39.235 --> 21:42.655
in a way that actually
threatens his primacy?"

21:42.739 --> 21:46.159
Ben decides, "Yes, I will take him out
to the cabin where Jacob is,

21:46.242 --> 21:50.580
and if Locke can see Jacob, I will know
there is something special about Locke.

21:50.663 --> 21:53.583
And if he can't,
then I can utterly dismiss him."

21:53.666 --> 21:57.545
[Lindelof] I also think, though, that
Ben has a certain confidence and swagger

21:57.629 --> 22:01.299
going into that cabin. I think that
he's probably about 95 percent sure

22:01.382 --> 22:03.510
that Locke will
not be able to see Jacob.

22:03.593 --> 22:08.848
Because when they first go in,
he says with such glee, it seems,

22:08.932 --> 22:12.810
restrained glee, but glee nonetheless,
"What, you can't see him?"

22:12.894 --> 22:16.814
I feel like he never would have brought
him out of there in the first place

22:16.898 --> 22:19.108
if he thought there
was a chance that he might.

22:19.192 --> 22:23.071
Then he seems incredibly unsettled
when he hears the words "help me."

22:23.154 --> 22:25.490
We need to talk
a bit about this stuff now,

22:25.573 --> 22:27.700
because once the scene comes,
it flies by.

22:27.784 --> 22:30.995
- [Cuse] Exactly.
- [Emerson] Yeah, Ben seems almost smug

22:31.079 --> 22:34.832
when he thinks that John
has not been anointed or chosen.

22:34.916 --> 22:39.295
[Cuse] Right. And so, obviously,
when you're preparing to do that scene,

22:39.379 --> 22:42.632
did you just kind of commit
to the notion that

22:42.715 --> 22:46.636
for all purposes, you were having a
conversation with that guy in the chair?

22:46.719 --> 22:47.971
What was your approach?

22:48.054 --> 22:50.640
[Emerson] Yeah, I tried... ll...

22:50.723 --> 22:56.062
I wrote out in my script...
Jacob's lines.

22:56.145 --> 22:59.065
- His unspoken or unheard lines.
- [Lindelof] Interesting.

22:59.148 --> 23:01.818
[Emerson] Responded to them
as if it were a conversation.

23:01.901 --> 23:04.445
[Lindelof] Like having
a conversation on a telephone,

23:04.529 --> 23:07.532
and the other actor is not present
to give it to you there.

23:07.615 --> 23:09.784
[Emerson] Yeah.
That kind of a conversation.

23:09.867 --> 23:13.246
[Cuse] What's interesting also is,
and this is a little Easter egg,

23:13.329 --> 23:17.709
is everybody sort of, they will take
sort of video frames from the show.

23:17.792 --> 23:22.255
One of the things that we had in
the show was Ben writing in his journal.

23:22.338 --> 23:25.091
And we were, like, "Wow." We didn't
actually approve or anoint those words.

23:25.091 --> 23:27.885
And we were, like, "Wow." We didn't
actually approve or anoint those words.

23:27.969 --> 23:31.598
And we actually had to take...
send the pages down from Hawaii,

23:31.681 --> 23:33.891
and Michael had written those pages.

23:33.975 --> 23:36.978
And we looked at them,
thinking that we would have to...

23:37.061 --> 23:40.773
We might want to rewrite or insert them.
Then once we read what Michael

23:40.857 --> 23:44.527
had written in his journal,
we were like, "That's totally dialed-in.

23:44.611 --> 23:47.614
Perfect. It's completely
within the character bandwidth

23:47.697 --> 23:51.242
- of what Ben would do." It was like...
- [Lindelof] It was fascinating.

23:51.326 --> 23:53.786
[Emerson] It was my little
literary improv on the set.

23:53.870 --> 23:56.414
- [Lindelof] Very nice.
- [Cuse] We had a conversation

23:56.497 --> 24:00.418
about how we might save work by farming,
like, five or six scripts out

24:00.501 --> 24:02.629
- to Michael next year.
- [Emerson laughs]

24:02.712 --> 24:06.424
[Lindelof] I actually love this moment.
This is sort of like the very beginnings

24:06.507 --> 24:09.594
of the Ben we have come to know
and love. He turns the fence off,

24:09.677 --> 24:13.264
but just to make sure,
he's gonna send the bunny through.

24:13.348 --> 24:18.102
His beloved pet. If it's not off,
what would have happened to that bunny?

24:18.186 --> 24:22.148
[Cuse] I'd like to kind of... I think
there's a lot to explore about Ben

24:22.231 --> 24:25.318
and his relationship with rabbits,
particularly what led him

24:25.401 --> 24:27.654
to start putting numbers on rabbits.

24:27.737 --> 24:30.615
[Lindelof] I have a suspicion
that it has something to do

24:30.698 --> 24:33.493
with fertility experiments,
but that's just me.

24:33.576 --> 24:36.454
[Emerson] It's not really malicious,
but it's awfully cold.

24:36.537 --> 24:39.123
- [Lindelof] It is.
- [Cuse] Easier to tell bunnies apart

24:39.207 --> 24:42.251
- if they're numbered, isn't it?
- [Lindelof] I would assume so.

24:42.335 --> 24:45.588
So here we come
to Ben's first meeting with Richard.

24:45.672 --> 24:49.008
Obviously this provoked
a significant amount of conversation

24:49.092 --> 24:54.263
in the fan community, in terms of
what kind of moisturizer Richard uses,

24:54.347 --> 24:58.184
or what sort of... what diet he's on.
Because he looks...

24:58.267 --> 25:02.689
He looks awfully young, considering.

25:02.772 --> 25:05.608
[Cuse] Which gets back
to the earlier line, where he says,

25:05.692 --> 25:08.152
"You do remember birthdays,
don't you, Richard?"

25:08.236 --> 25:10.947
- Maybe it has to do with that.
- [Lindelof] There he is.

25:11.030 --> 25:15.326
One of the things that I love
about this story is finding out that Ben

25:15.410 --> 25:19.539
is actually not born on the island,
makes him instantly more accessible

25:19.622 --> 25:23.626
to the audience in a way that I'm
not sure he was before learning that.

25:23.710 --> 25:28.381
And as the show progresses, you're
seeing that the island is a lot like

25:28.464 --> 25:32.385
the United States of America in that
everyone who claims to be

25:32.468 --> 25:36.389
an actual American
is in fact anything but.

25:36.472 --> 25:38.391
[Emerson] That's true.

25:38.474 --> 25:41.894
[Lindelof] As you dig deeper into
the show, you realize that there was

25:41.978 --> 25:45.022
an indigenous population
on the island at some point,

25:45.106 --> 25:48.735
but we don't even know for sure
when Alpert came to the island.

25:48.818 --> 25:53.072
And the deeper that you go, the more you
find people have been migrating there

25:53.156 --> 25:57.618
from the world that we all know. That
makes it more grounded in reality,

25:57.702 --> 26:00.413
as opposed to crazy town.

26:00.496 --> 26:04.751
[Emerson] This was a wonderful,
dangerous scene, I thought.

26:04.834 --> 26:07.128
- [Cuse] Yeah. It...
- [Lindelof] What do you...?

26:07.211 --> 26:11.299
- What do you mean by dangerous?
- [Emerson] I just thought there was...

26:11.382 --> 26:13.718
The boy's in jeopardy here.

26:13.801 --> 26:17.764
He's out in the jungle
with this scary stranger,

26:17.847 --> 26:23.603
and I thought the conversation
was really nicely layered and loaded.

26:23.686 --> 26:27.482
[Lindelof] What's interesting is that
Alpert seems so particularly focused

26:27.565 --> 26:32.028
on the fact that Ben's mother died
off the island, and he saw her here.

26:32.111 --> 26:34.822
That piece of information
seems so significant to him.

26:34.906 --> 26:38.201
[Cuse] Although, we wanted to make it
very clear that it wasn't at all

26:38.284 --> 26:41.579
a surprise to Alpert, and that
was what really engaged Ben.

26:41.662 --> 26:46.125
That, basically, this is a piece
of information that if he told anyone

26:46.209 --> 26:48.294
in Dharma, they would be skeptical

26:48.377 --> 26:50.630
that he saw a vision of his mother,

26:50.713 --> 26:54.634
whereas Alpert's attitude
is one of kind of concern,

26:54.717 --> 26:56.886
and of trying to
find out some more facts.

26:56.969 --> 27:00.973
But the subtext is that he completely
believes Ben saw his mother.

27:01.057 --> 27:04.852
[Lindelof] There's also this...
Are you familiar with the story

27:04.936 --> 27:08.105
- of the Panchen Lama, Michael?
- No.

27:08.189 --> 27:11.943
Basically, the Panchen Lama's job
is to identify the new Dalai Lama.

27:12.026 --> 27:15.154
So when the Dalai Lama dies,
they go to the Panchen Lama,

27:15.238 --> 27:18.574
and the Panchen Lama goes
and meditates and basically figures out

27:18.658 --> 27:22.161
where the next Dalai Lama
has been reincarnated into.

27:22.245 --> 27:25.748
When the Panchen Lama dies, the
Dalai Lama finds the next Panchen Lama,

27:25.832 --> 27:28.709
which sets up this
"chicken or the egg" scenario.

27:28.793 --> 27:31.838
And I think this is relevant
before we go into the cabin,

27:31.921 --> 27:35.424
because in many ways, I think
Richard Alpert might be construed

27:35.508 --> 27:39.095
as a Panchen Lama, as he has
this conversation with this boy

27:39.178 --> 27:41.639
who sees something
he wasn't supposed to see.

27:41.722 --> 27:45.101
And now I think Ben finds himself
in the role of the Panchen Lama

27:45.184 --> 27:49.313
as he brings Locke into this cabin
in an attempt to sort of determine

27:49.397 --> 27:54.610
whether or not the island has chosen him
to become its new surrogate.

27:54.694 --> 27:58.614
[Cuse] Now, in this scene, one of
the things that I think we kind of take

27:58.698 --> 28:02.285
as a matter of fact is that people
watch this show, they tape the show,

28:02.368 --> 28:06.414
they DVR the show.
And so when people watched the show,

28:06.497 --> 28:09.625
they were like, "That was all crazy.
I didn't see anything."

28:09.709 --> 28:14.839
But in fact, yes, there are several
pieces of Jacob. You can see them.

28:14.922 --> 28:19.510
But you can't really see them
unless you stop and tape the thing,

28:19.594 --> 28:22.305
and look at it slowly.
So it was interesting for us

28:22.388 --> 28:25.725
to see how the audience
reacted to this scene.

28:25.808 --> 28:29.020
When it played, a lot of people
were like, "I didn't get it.

28:29.103 --> 28:31.647
What was going on there?"
There was nobody there.

28:31.731 --> 28:34.859
Other people very clearly stopped,
taped it, looked,

28:34.942 --> 28:36.944
saw that there
were images of Jacob.

28:37.028 --> 28:41.908
And we tried to actually add one other
image that would be visible clearly

28:41.991 --> 28:45.536
for the audience, this close-up
of an eye, which kind of goes back to...

28:45.620 --> 28:47.872
...kind of thematically
was unified with

28:47.955 --> 28:51.834
this image of eyes that started really
from the very beginning of the pilot.

28:51.918 --> 28:56.464
But I'm not sure people really
understood that that was Jacob's eye.

28:56.547 --> 29:01.218
And it's kind of interesting that this
was a scene that required sort of a...

29:01.302 --> 29:05.473
...active participation on the part of
the audience to really get its full...

29:05.556 --> 29:08.434
To get its full impact
or to have a full understanding

29:08.517 --> 29:12.438
- of what you've seen in this scene.
- [Lindelof] We're not gonna reveal here

29:12.521 --> 29:17.276
exactly who plays Jacob, but obviously
the message boards were alight with,

29:17.360 --> 29:19.445
"My God, that's Terry O'Quinn in a wig.

29:19.528 --> 29:23.115
Wait, that's Christian Shephard.
Wait, it's Emerson."

29:23.199 --> 29:28.746
So, who actually played Jacob is not
something we're going to reveal.

29:28.829 --> 29:32.208
But if you watch very closely,

29:32.291 --> 29:36.087
after Locke hears the words "help me,"
there you go.

29:36.170 --> 29:40.633
How long... Did you guys do this all in
one day, this entire scene, Michael?

29:40.716 --> 29:44.845
- [Emerson] The interior of it, yeah.
- [Lindelof] Shot on a sound stage?

29:44.929 --> 29:49.642
- [Emerson] It was. It was a long day.
- [Lindelof] Yeah, I'll bet. I'll bet.

29:49.725 --> 29:55.106
[Cuse] And there just seems to be...
One of the things that...

29:55.189 --> 29:58.484
...that I love are just kind of
the scenes of you and Terry.

29:58.567 --> 30:01.028
There just really seems,
on an actor level,

30:01.112 --> 30:03.531
a real connection
between the two of you,

30:03.614 --> 30:07.576
and the two of you kind of really
fire off each other in ways that are...

30:07.660 --> 30:10.329
...think just elevate
the content of the scene,

30:10.413 --> 30:13.457
every time we have a scene
with the two of you guys together.

30:13.541 --> 30:17.628
[Emerson] Somehow,
we have a natural, sort of,

30:17.712 --> 30:20.464
electrical connection, he and ll.

30:20.548 --> 30:24.760
[Lindelof] I think that Ben really
knows how to push Locke's buttons.

30:24.844 --> 30:28.681
Again, the pun is intended.
But going back to season one,

30:28.764 --> 30:32.351
he looks at Locke and basically says,
"I was coming for you, John."

30:32.435 --> 30:33.477
[Emerson] Yeah.

30:33.561 --> 30:35.813
[Lindelof] He knows,
whether that sentiment

30:35.896 --> 30:39.942
is actually true or not, that that's
the beginning of their relationship.

30:40.026 --> 30:43.946
That is the hook that sort of
leads us all the way to this cabin.

30:44.030 --> 30:48.743
It's very possible that Jacob
in fact did send Ben to go and find out,

30:48.826 --> 30:52.580
or bring back John Locke,
when he was going out there.

30:52.663 --> 30:56.208
We know he also began to have
this issue with the tumor in his back.

30:56.292 --> 31:00.212
So those two issues might've been...
sort of balancing each other out.

31:00.296 --> 31:03.340
Here's this guy who he may
or may not have been coming for,

31:03.424 --> 31:05.718
and is certainly threatened by.

31:05.801 --> 31:09.013
And what I love about
their little dance is, you know,

31:09.096 --> 31:11.849
it always feels
like Locke has the advantage,

31:11.932 --> 31:14.685
and then somehow
Ben turns it around on him.

31:14.769 --> 31:18.481
- And then... and then here we go.
- [Jacob] Help me.

31:22.026 --> 31:24.070
- [Emerson] Say what?
- [Lindelof] Yeah.

31:24.153 --> 31:26.989
Instantly... I love Terry,
like, "What did you say?"

31:27.073 --> 31:30.367
But he's playing as he knows
that Ben didn't say it.

31:30.451 --> 31:34.538
And whatever was said,
you clearly didn't hear it.

31:34.622 --> 31:37.416
I heard you. You said...

31:37.500 --> 31:40.753
[Emerson] And here we go.
Jacob gets upset.

31:42.463 --> 31:43.798
[Lindelof] Sure does.

31:44.590 --> 31:46.926
[Emerson] Things start
flying through the air.

31:47.009 --> 31:50.137
[Lindelof] It's after you grab
his shoulders and he throws you back

31:50.221 --> 31:51.639
that if you look very closely,

31:51.722 --> 31:54.892
as the camera pans back over,
you will see something.

31:57.561 --> 32:00.106
That's enough!
You've had your fun!

32:00.773 --> 32:02.108
[Lindelof] And there he is.

32:02.191 --> 32:05.361
[Cuse] There he was. You can
see him there. And there's the eye.

32:06.195 --> 32:09.323
So you have little pieces,
but you don't really get...

32:09.406 --> 32:11.367
The audience watches the first time,

32:11.450 --> 32:17.248
they're so full of other emotions, that
registering those images is hard to do.

32:17.331 --> 32:19.583
[Emerson] It's good.
It's nice and ambiguous.

32:19.667 --> 32:23.546
[Lindelof] I love this moment here, that
you play, where you basically sort of

32:23.629 --> 32:25.965
come out looking shell-shocked.

32:26.048 --> 32:29.051
But it doesn't stop you
from hanging up the lantern.

32:29.135 --> 32:31.762
It's just a very
nice piece of business.

32:31.846 --> 32:34.098
[Lindelof chuckles]

32:34.181 --> 32:38.310
[Cuse] It's interesting because
we're always very conscious

32:38.394 --> 32:42.148
of the ease with which one can fall

32:42.231 --> 32:45.818
into writing a character like Ben
as being arch.

32:45.901 --> 32:49.947
It's kind of the classic sort of
movie villain kind of a thing.

32:50.030 --> 32:53.993
I think that we try
to work at making sure

32:54.076 --> 32:58.164
that we sort of stay this side
of that line.

32:58.247 --> 33:00.749
We also benefit
from your talents as an actor

33:00.749 --> 33:02.877
We also benefit
from your talents as an actor

33:02.960 --> 33:05.171
to kind of keep us
on this side of the line.

33:05.254 --> 33:08.757
We were talking about that line
we dropped about the birthday cake,

33:08.841 --> 33:11.510
- that we...
- [Emerson] Sure.

33:11.594 --> 33:15.306
[Cuse] That we dropped. There are
certain places where we can't tell

33:15.389 --> 33:18.809
until we edit the shows
whether these lines are gonna play.

33:18.893 --> 33:24.481
Or... We're... You try certain things
and you let them play in the script,

33:24.565 --> 33:26.942
and you see whether they work
or they don't work.

33:27.026 --> 33:30.029
We benefit from the fact
that Michael is extremely good

33:30.112 --> 33:35.534
at sort of delivering dialogue
that sometimes is a little spun,

33:35.618 --> 33:41.207
but delivering it in a way that seems
real as opposed to mustache-twirling.

33:41.290 --> 33:44.793
[Lindelof] Speaking of mustaches,
I'm still waiting for the milk people

33:44.877 --> 33:49.506
to contact us from Michael's line
in season one, "Got any milk?"

33:49.590 --> 33:51.217
- [Cuse] Yeah.
- [Emerson] Come on.

33:51.300 --> 33:55.346
[Lindelof] Put the milk mustache on him,
have him sit there in his Dharma suit

33:55.429 --> 33:57.723
- next to his dead father.
- [Cuse] I'm gonna say

33:57.806 --> 34:00.768
- they're not gonna contact us.
- [Lindelof] I'm still waiting.

34:00.851 --> 34:03.854
[Cuse] I'm gonna say that
their Milk Advisory Board is not sure

34:03.938 --> 34:06.857
that's the best image for milk.
Not that it wouldn't...

34:06.941 --> 34:10.986
Not that that wasn't one of the best
lines of the entire series, really.

34:11.070 --> 34:13.322
[Emerson] That's my
favorite funny scene.

34:13.405 --> 34:15.199
[Lindelof] That was a classic.

34:15.282 --> 34:19.578
[Emerson] That villainous archness
you talk about, it's like a glue trap.

34:19.662 --> 34:24.166
Once you step on it,
it's hard to step back off of it.

34:24.250 --> 34:29.755
So I appreciate
the way you look after that in post.

34:29.838 --> 34:33.509
[Cuse] And I think you look after it,
also, in the performance.

34:33.592 --> 34:35.970
I think it's that combination.

34:36.053 --> 34:39.807
You know, you want your villain
to be colorful, you want...

34:39.890 --> 34:43.435
You want the character, in a
certain way... We were actually doing

34:43.519 --> 34:47.106
an interview yesterday, talking
about who does each of us like to write.

34:47.189 --> 34:50.609
We were talking about how great it is
to write Ben because Ben is...

34:50.693 --> 34:55.114
In a certain way, he possesses
a certain kind of range of behavior

34:55.197 --> 34:58.409
as a character that's kind
of broader than some of the others

34:58.492 --> 35:02.538
because he is capable
of sort of any kind of action.

35:02.621 --> 35:04.957
Actions that are sort of shocking,

35:05.040 --> 35:08.544
and also actions that
are kind of in the normal bandwidth

35:08.627 --> 35:11.922
of what a character does. Others
are more traditionally heroic.

35:12.006 --> 35:14.133
They won't do certain things.

35:14.216 --> 35:19.179
That's... And I think that we haven't
gotten anywhere near to the place

35:19.263 --> 35:23.892
where Ben doesn't continue
to surprise us as a character.

35:23.976 --> 35:26.437
In terms of writing someone,
that's fun,

35:26.520 --> 35:30.149
because you really... As an audience
you're always in a state of unease.

35:30.232 --> 35:32.901
You don't know what Ben
is gonna do in any situation.

35:32.985 --> 35:38.324
He has this sort of very, kind of calm,

35:38.407 --> 35:43.787
and a certain civility to him, and yet,
whether it's something as subtle

35:43.871 --> 35:47.333
as manipulating
Elizabeth Mitchell's character,

35:47.416 --> 35:51.295
or in terms of kind of trying
to keep her on the island,

35:51.378 --> 35:54.006
and that flashback story, or here.

35:54.089 --> 35:58.344
This appears to be really a kind
of warm and fuzzy father-son moment.

35:58.427 --> 36:00.804
They're gonna drive up
and have a few beers.

36:00.888 --> 36:03.891
Well, that's not exactly
what's going on.

36:03.974 --> 36:07.519
[Lindelof] That's what I love about him,
too, is once his mind is made up

36:07.603 --> 36:10.356
about something,
he just goes ahead and does it.

36:10.439 --> 36:13.484
It's obvious in this scene
where he's looking at his watch,

36:13.567 --> 36:18.739
as he's zipping up his jumpsuit there,
that he knows what he's going to do.

36:18.822 --> 36:23.577
And, you know, even though his father
has this sweet moment with him

36:23.660 --> 36:27.498
and apologizes genuinely
for forgetting his birthday, it's done.

36:27.581 --> 36:30.042
- He's already... It's done.
- [Emerson] It is done.

36:30.125 --> 36:34.296
[Cuse] After this scene, Michael,
I've got your birthday on my calendar.

36:34.380 --> 36:37.716
- 1 will be calling you every year.
- [Emerson] Absolutely.

36:37.800 --> 36:40.344
This is a chilly one here.

36:42.638 --> 36:45.140
- Boy.
- [Lindelof] It sure is.

36:45.224 --> 36:49.395
But the moment that I particularly love,
in terms of your performance

36:49.478 --> 36:52.189
throughout this sequence,
is the look on your face

36:52.272 --> 36:56.527
when you pull your gas mask off after
the damage is done, once you see Alpert.

36:56.610 --> 37:02.366
That's the first time that you
really see the effect of this action.

37:02.449 --> 37:05.744
And we don't know at the time
of watching this whether or not this

37:05.828 --> 37:08.205
was all Ben's idea
or somebody else's idea,

37:08.288 --> 37:12.209
or if he's their leader yet, or kind
of where they are in his ascension.

37:12.292 --> 37:16.505
Only that he is a willing
and active participant, you know,

37:16.588 --> 37:20.259
in essentially this...
you know, mass genocide.

37:20.342 --> 37:23.387
[Cuse] Yeah.
We wanted this flashback story...

37:23.470 --> 37:25.222
They're sort of chaptered out.

37:25.305 --> 37:29.184
And this was sort of the birth of Ben
becoming the guy that he is.

37:29.268 --> 37:31.895
We wanted this story
to help the audience,

37:31.979 --> 37:33.730
first of all, really understand

37:33.814 --> 37:38.861
what exactly happened vis-a-vis
the Dharma Initiative and the Others.

37:38.944 --> 37:42.865
And we get that answer to that.
We discover with complete certainty

37:42.948 --> 37:46.660
that the Others
purged the Dharma Initiative,

37:46.743 --> 37:50.956
like maybe many other groups on the
island who kind of ran into the Others.

37:51.039 --> 37:55.419
The Others win, and they lose.
And... But...

37:55.502 --> 37:59.256
[Lindelof] One second. I love the look
on Michael's face. He can't look.

37:59.339 --> 38:02.134
He's sitting there,
but he doesn't want to watch.

38:02.217 --> 38:03.260
[Cuse] Yeah.

38:03.343 --> 38:06.054
[Emerson] This was a terrifying
scene to shoot.

38:06.138 --> 38:07.890
[Lindelof] What were you thinking?

38:07.973 --> 38:10.267
[Emerson] His death throes
were so palpable.

38:10.350 --> 38:13.979
I mean, he was very convincing.
It was terrifying.

38:14.062 --> 38:15.564
[Lindelof] Awesome actor.

38:15.647 --> 38:19.109
[Cuse] Yeah. And here,
there was this kind of great mix

38:19.193 --> 38:22.112
of sort of shock at the sight

38:22.196 --> 38:24.656
of all these people being dead.

38:24.740 --> 38:27.659
And I love the kind of
real subtlety and complexity

38:27.743 --> 38:31.497
of Ben's emotions,
even hidden behind the gas mask,

38:31.580 --> 38:35.751
as he sort of takes in the, kind of
the horror of what he has wrought.

38:35.834 --> 38:39.588
And at the same time, there's also
kind of a complete acceptance of it.

38:39.671 --> 38:43.884
The chapter that we don't know yet
is what actually provoked this in Ben.

38:43.967 --> 38:49.848
What allowed him
to basically transform from a guy

38:49.932 --> 38:54.561
who was a member of this society
to someone who would be really

38:54.645 --> 38:58.273
- kind of a catalyst for this purge.
- [Lindelof] It's interesting...

38:58.357 --> 39:01.235
[Emerson] He was more than
a passive participant.

39:01.318 --> 39:02.819
When we first shot it,

39:02.903 --> 39:06.406
they didn't have me opening up
that canister of poison in the van.

39:06.490 --> 39:08.158
[Cuse] That was added in post.

39:08.242 --> 39:11.578
And I think it was good and
there was a certain amount of confusion.

39:11.662 --> 39:14.373
Sometimes you don't
get story points as clear,

39:14.456 --> 39:16.166
and there was a lot of confusion,

39:16.250 --> 39:21.880
"Is this gas island-wide? How does what
is happening over in New Otherton

39:21.964 --> 39:24.841
<i>relate 10 what's happening
in this Dharma van?"</i>

39:24.925 --> 39:30.889
We came up with this idea that we would
just have a gas canister that would...

39:30.973 --> 39:34.893
And so we shot an insert
of Ben pulling that gas canister,

39:34.977 --> 39:38.397
so that we would understand
that the two events were simultaneous,

39:38.480 --> 39:41.608
but were not...
It wasn't the exact same chemical,

39:41.692 --> 39:44.236
so there wasn't
a gas cloud over the whole island.

39:44.319 --> 39:47.447
[Lindelof] And we felt that the spirit
of the idea was the same,

39:47.531 --> 39:50.784
which is that Ben is still responsible
for his father's death,

39:50.867 --> 39:54.955
and knows that he's gonna die. Here's
that look I was telling you about.

39:55.038 --> 39:58.375
[Cuse] In a certain way, in actuality,
it made Ben more responsible,

39:58.458 --> 40:00.085
because he fired the canister.

40:00.168 --> 40:03.755
It wasn't just, like,
"Drive somebody to a place and park,

40:03.839 --> 40:07.884
and at 4:00, something bad is gonna
happen." He had to initiate the action.

40:07.968 --> 40:11.847
[Emerson] It was a sin of commission,
rather than omission.

40:11.930 --> 40:14.933
[Cuse] And here,
this is kind of the scene where

40:15.017 --> 40:19.605
we really see that maybe, in the way
that Damon was talking about earlier,

40:19.688 --> 40:23.650
Ben is being anointed as perhaps
being the future leader of this group,

40:23.734 --> 40:28.071
because Alpert
has sort of singled him out.

40:28.155 --> 40:32.826
[Lindelof] Michael, when do you think
that Ben decided to pop Locke here?

40:34.536 --> 40:38.790
[Emerson] Very soon. About the time
the sun rose this day, I think.

40:38.874 --> 40:42.544
- [Lindelof] Exactly.
- [Cuse] Yeah. Our idea was once Ben

40:42.628 --> 40:44.796
gets confirmation
that Locke can see Ben,

40:44.880 --> 40:47.674
- he has no choice but to eliminate...
- [Lindelof] See Jacob.

40:47.758 --> 40:51.136
[Cuse] I mean see Jacob. He has
no choice but to eliminate Locke.

40:51.219 --> 40:53.889
- Locke now becomes a threat.
- [Lindelof] But I find that

40:53.972 --> 40:56.558
Ben makes an interesting choice,
which is that he

40:56.642 --> 41:00.896
gut-shoots him and leaves him there,
and literally says to him,

41:00.979 --> 41:04.691
"Let's see if he can help you."
He doesn't shoot him in the head.

41:04.775 --> 41:07.361
He very well could make sure
that Locke is dead.

41:07.444 --> 41:12.157
But it's almost like he's offering up
kind of one final test, just to make...

41:12.240 --> 41:14.117
[Emerson] Yeah, maybe it is a test.

41:14.201 --> 41:17.788
[Cuse] I just interpreted it
as part of a kind of a measure of,

41:17.871 --> 41:21.333
sort of, his sadistic nature

41:21.416 --> 41:25.003
that, like, he'll let Locke
have a slow, painful death,

41:25.087 --> 41:28.674
and he's not gonna bother
to put him out of his misery easily.

41:28.757 --> 41:31.802
[Emerson] Yeah, a few minutes
to meditate on his fate.

41:31.885 --> 41:34.888
- [Lindelof] Exactly.
- [Cuse] I think that Ben walks away

41:34.971 --> 41:38.058
thinking Locke's got 20 minutes
to bleed out, and that's it.

41:38.141 --> 41:40.811
Then he'll be a corpse too.

41:40.894 --> 41:42.312
Help me.

41:42.771 --> 41:44.981
John...

41:45.065 --> 41:48.026
[Lindelof] He really wants to know,
though, what Jacob said.

41:48.110 --> 41:50.737
And that's what's
so interesting about it.

41:50.821 --> 41:52.489
[Emerson] That's what's crucial.

41:52.572 --> 41:55.784
[Cuse] And the "help me" is
obviously very carefully chosen.

41:55.867 --> 41:57.869
And, I think, between the circle of ash

41:57.953 --> 42:03.250
and the sort of sense of,
kind of, containment in that cabin,

42:03.333 --> 42:08.004
and Ben's sort of lack
of certainty of his own role,

42:08.088 --> 42:11.341
I think it's kind of clear
that things are...

42:11.425 --> 42:15.303
...things are unsettled
in Others' leadership-ville here.

42:15.387 --> 42:18.557
[Lindelof] And I think Ben seems
so amused, almost, in a dark way,

42:18.640 --> 42:21.852
that that's what Locke heard.
It's sort of, like, "Help me?

42:21.935 --> 42:25.939
- He needs your help? Oh, my God."
- [Cuse] Wow. That flew by.

42:26.022 --> 42:28.275
- [Lindelof] It sure did.
- [Emerson] It sure did.

42:28.358 --> 42:30.777
- [Cuse] Well, Michael...
- [Lindelof] Hope we let you talk a bit.

42:30.777 --> 42:31.361
- [Cuse] Well, Michael...
- [Lindelof] Hope we let you talk a bit.

42:31.445 --> 42:33.572
- [Cuse] We talked a lot.
- [Lindelof] Sorry.

42:33.655 --> 42:35.407
[Emerson laughs]

42:35.490 --> 42:38.952
[Cuse] Anyway, it was fun to get
together and rehash this with you.

42:39.035 --> 42:41.913
[Emerson] Yeah. Great spending
time with you, no matter what.

42:41.997 --> 42:43.749
[Lindelof] The pleasure is all ours.

42:43.832 --> 42:47.961
And we look forward to writing some
awesome stuff for you in season four.

42:48.044 --> 42:52.090
You know, probably at the time
that this DVD is actually available,

42:52.174 --> 42:54.760
you'll already have played
a significant chunk of it.

42:54.843 --> 42:56.928
[Emerson] We'll be digging
into it already.

42:57.012 --> 42:59.556
- [Lindelof] Exactly.
- [Emerson] I look forward to it.

42:59.639 --> 43:03.226
[Cuse] Thank you all for listening,
and we'll talk to you some time soon.

43:03.310 --> 43:05.771
- [Emerson] Bye-bye.
- [Lindelof] Bye.
